// Star Wars: R2-D2, built part by part from published measurements and the plans that agree with them, and Luke's
// X-wing, Red Five, measured from photographs of the studio model (its own section, below, says how).
//
//     import 'star-wars' { R2D2, XWing }
//
//     object R2D2()             // standing on his two outer legs
//     object R2D2(3, 40)        // on three, as he rolls, his dome turned 40 degrees to his right
//     object R2D2(2, 0, 0.6)    // after a few days in the desert
//     object XWing()            // Red Five, its S-foils locked in attack position
//     object XWing(0, 1, 1)     // folded, standing on its gear, the canopy up
//
// | `R2D2` | The astromech droid: dome, body, the two outer legs and the center leg and foot. |
// | `XWing` | Red Five, the T-65B X-wing of A New Hope, in meters, R2-D2 in its socket, a pilot under its glass. |
//
// **`legs` is two or three.**  On two he stands upright with his center foot tucked up under him; on
// three his body tips back eighteen degrees about his shoulders -- the angle builders set, there being no
// published figure -- his outer legs lean fifteen the other way about the pivots in their feet, their
// feet going back and his shoulders down, and his center leg runs out along his body until its foot stands
// flat on the ground just ahead of the other two.  **`dome` turns his dome**, in the scene's unit of angle,
// from facing straight ahead; positive turns it to his right, as `rotate Y` would.  **`weathered` is how long he
// has been out**, from nought, as clean as the day he was finished, to one: grime in his panel seams and round his
// fittings, sand settled on everything that faces up and kicked up over his feet, and the blue and white worn
// through to the aluminum on his edges -- each of his own materials weathered where it is, by `weather { }`.
//
// **It is written in inches, and a scene unit IS an inch**, as the furniture is: every published figure
// for him is in inches, so the numbers here can be checked against them directly.  A scene in meters
// scales him by 0.0254.  **He stands on `y = 0` and faces `-Z`**, his legs to either side on X.
//
// -- Where the shape comes from -----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The published measurements, which the R2 Builders Club took from a screen-used unit and builders have
// repeated since: the dome is 18.25 in across; the body a little over 18 in (46.3 cm, from the Starlog
// Technical Journal's plans, by ArndToo's reckoning at r2-d2.de) and about 21.5 in tall; the legs 31 in
// tall, 8.75 in wide at the top and 4.5 in at the middle seen from the side, and 3 in thick from the
// front; and the whole of him 42.5 to 43 in (107.8 cm by the 1977 Norank prints and a Smithsonian unit;
// 1.09 m on StarWars.com).
//
// Where those say nothing, the proportions are a front elevation on graph paper with a metric scale,
// which scales by the dome to 17.9 pixels to the inch and then agrees with every measurement above to
// within two percent -- 43.0 in overall, the legs 31.5, 2.7 thick -- and so is trusted for the rest:
// the body 18.3 across, the dome's band from 32.4 and its ring below that, the legs' place either side,
// and the feet, 7.2 wide.  A cutaway in the style of the cross-section books shows the colors and the
// details, and a photograph of a finished droid from his front and right the pistons on his legs and the
// depth of his blue.
//
// The side comes from the starboard elevation of the 1977 production drawings (20th Century-Fox, sheet 4
// of 4), which scales by the dome to 33.97 pixels to the inch: the body's barrel running down to 10.55 in,
// a flat step in from its edge and the skirt narrowing from there to its bottom; the leg's round top
// centered on its shoulder hub, narrowing in a V to its shank and widening again at its ankle; and the
// foot, a long base under a shorter top, with the panel sunk in its side and the block and slots on its
// wheel's housing.  Its heights are taken a fiftieth larger, the drawings standing
// 42.2 in to the finished droid's 43.  Those drawings are of an early unit, so where the finished droid
// differs the finished droid wins: the leg's widths are the published ones, and the foot is as long,
// against the body, as a five-view drawing of the finished droid makes it (Onno van Braam,
// the-blueprints.com, 1090 mm tall) -- 12.6 in where the early drawing has 14.  A line drawing of him on
// three legs shows how the center leg stands.  None of these is reproduced here.

// -- Materials ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The dome is polished aluminum; everything else is painted, white and blue.
R2Aluminum = material { metal 'aluminum'  roughness 0.18 }
R2White = material { pigment [0.86, 0.86, 0.84]  specular 0.4  shininess 60  reflective 0.04  fresnel }
R2Blue = material { pigment [0.01, 0.055, 0.45]  specular 0.4  shininess 60  reflective 0.04  fresnel }
R2Dark = material { pigment [0.12, 0.12, 0.13]  specular 0.3  shininess 40 }
// The gray metal of the small fittings, steel, mirrors sharply: blurred, its sixteen looks at every point cost
// a quarter of the droid's render and showed nothing on parts this small.
R2Metal = material { metal 'iron' }
R2Gray = material { pigment [0.42, 0.42, 0.43]  specular 0.3  shininess 40 }
R2Satin = material { pigment [0.84, 0.85, 0.87]  metallic 0.3  diffuse 0.8  specular 0.6  shininess 80 }
R2Hole = material { pigment [0.025, 0.025, 0.03]  diffuse 0.5  specular 0.1 }
R2Brass = material { metal 'brass' }
// The radar eye's lens: black glass under a coating a quarter of a green wave thick, which mirrors less than
// bare glass would and what it does mirror, purple, as a coated camera lens does.
R2EyeLens = material {
    pigment [0.012, 0.012, 0.016]  diffuse 0.3  specular 0.9  shininess 300
    interior { ior 1.52 }
    film { thickness 110  ior 1.38 }
}
// The lights in the dome, glowing: the logic displays' blue and white lamps, and those that are off, and the
// front indicator's frosted blue lens.  They glow by `emission`, the same under any lamps or none, and so
// light what is close to them in a scene that follows bounced light.
R2LampBlue = material { pigment [0.3, 0.5, 1]  emission 3  diffuse 0.2  specular 0.3 }
R2LampWhite = material { pigment [0.95, 0.97, 1]  emission 2.5  diffuse 0.2  specular 0.3 }
R2LampOff = material { pigment [0.06, 0.07, 0.09]  specular 0.4  shininess 80 }
R2IndicatorBlue = material { pigment [0.45, 0.68, 1]  emission 1.5  diffuse 0.5  specular 0.7  shininess 150 }
R2IndicatorGreen = material { pigment [0.5, 1, 0.68]  emission 1.5  diffuse 0.5  specular 0.7  shininess 150 }
R2LampGreen = material { pigment [0.35, 1, 0.45]  emission 2.8  diffuse 0.2  specular 0.3 }
R2Chrome = material { metal 'chromium' }

// -- The dome ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The hemisphere's middle and radius -- 18.25 across, its crown at 43 -- and the bottom of the upright band
// it stands on, as wide as the body and as tall as from the body's top to the hemisphere's middle.
R2DomeMiddle = 33.875
R2DomeRadius = 9.125
R2DomeBase = 31.5

// How far the radar eye's face leans back from upright, and the point on it at the middle of the lens.
R2EyeLean = 30
R2EyeAt = [0.05, 39, -7.95]

// A piece of the dome's skin between its shape -- the hemisphere and the band under it -- grown or shrunk to
// `inner` and to `outer`: between the meridians `from` and
// `to`, each drawn in toward the other by `inset`, and between the heights `low` and `high`.  A meridian is
// measured in degrees from straight ahead, toward his left (+X) as it grows.  The last box only holds the
// piece tight, so that a ray that does not come near it is spared it.
primitive R2DomeRegion(from, to, low, high, inset, inner, outer) -> group {
    middle = (from + to) / 2
    half = (to - from) / 2
    across = outer * sin(half degrees) + 0.2
    rise = max(high - R2DomeMiddle, 0)
    near = sqrt(inner * inner - rise * rise) * cos(half degrees) - 0.2
    return group {
        intersection {
            difference {
                union {
                    sphere { scale outer  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
                    cylinder { min Y R2DomeBase - 0.1  max Y R2DomeMiddle  scale [outer, 1, outer] }
                }
                union {
                    sphere { scale inner  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
                    cylinder { min Y R2DomeBase - 0.2  max Y R2DomeMiddle  scale [inner, 1, inner] }
                }
            }
            cube { scale 12  translate [inset + 12, 38, 0]  rotate Y -from }
            cube { scale 12  translate [-inset - 12, 38, 0]  rotate Y -to }
            cube {
                scale [across, (high - low) / 2, (outer + 0.2 - near) / 2]
                translate [0, (high + low) / 2, -(outer + 0.2 + near) / 2]
                rotate Y -middle
            }
        }
    }
}

// A panel let into the dome: the piece itself, 0.06 thick and flush with the skin, and the cut it sits in,
// to be taken out of the dome -- 0.05 wider all round, so that a dark seam shows round the panel.
primitive R2DomePanel(from, to, low, high, inset = 0) -> group {
    return group { object R2DomeRegion(from, to, low, high, inset, R2DomeRadius - 0.06, R2DomeRadius) }
}
primitive R2DomeCut(from, to, low, high, inset = 0) -> group {
    return group {
        object R2DomeRegion(
            from, to, low - 0.05, high + 0.05, inset - 0.05, R2DomeRadius - 0.06, R2DomeRadius + 0.2
        )
    }
}

// Where the dome's panels are, each as (from, to) in degrees round from straight ahead, toward his left as it
// grows.  The lower row runs from 33.35 to 36.5 all round, but for the front indicator's panel, which stops
// at 35.8 under the radar eye -- the front elevation's heights, which the 1977 side elevation agrees with to
// a fifth of an inch -- and the pie panels on top from 40.9 to 42.5, six of them between spokes 0.35 wide,
// one spoke straight ahead, round a blue cap over 42.7 with a ring of bare dome between.
//
// The places round the dome come from photographs of a fan-made droid from in front, from his left and from
// behind, each registered by fitting the camera that took it to the dome's own stripe, its pie ring and the
// spoke straight ahead or straight behind; and from one of the finished droid from his front and right.
// From in front: the front indicator's panel, a little to his left as the front elevation also draws it,
// then to his right a narrow panel, the bare frame of the logic displays, another narrow panel and the long
// panel down his right side, which the 1977 side elevation ends at -90; to his left the bare dome the
// holoprojector stands on, then the first of the narrow panels down his left side.  From his left, those
// and the rest of that side -- three narrow panels, a wide one, a blue frame round a bare inset with a small
// square over it, and a bare panel -- set between where the front and the back put its ends.  From behind:
// the back of that bare panel, the bare frame round the rear indicator's blue panel just to his left of
// straight behind, the bare dome the rear holoprojector stands on, and the rear logic display's panel with
// a narrow strip before it, which the 1977 side elevation ends at -97.
R2DomeBluePanels = group {
    object R2DomePanel(-6.5, 17.8, 33.35, 35.8)
    object R2DomePanel(-14.5, -8, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(-31, -16, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(-40, -32.5, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(-93, -42, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(-153.5, -97, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(-159.5, -155, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(154.2, 178, 33.75, 36.15)
    difference {
        object R2DomePanel(104.5, 115.3, 33.35, 36)
        object R2DomeCut(106.5, 113.3, 33.7, 35.65)
    }
    object R2DomePanel(108, 115.3, 36.3, 37.1)
    object R2DomePanel(80, 103, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(67, 78.3, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(54.5, 65, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(39, 52, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomePanel(20.5, 26.5, 38.3, 40.4)
    object R2DomePanel(20.5, 26, 36, 37.9)
    for k in [0, 5] {
        object R2DomePanel(k * 60, k * 60 + 60, 40.9, 42.5, 0.175)
    }
    intersection {
        difference {
            sphere { scale R2DomeRadius  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
            sphere { scale R2DomeRadius - 0.06  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
        }
        cube { scale [3, 0.5, 3]  translate Y 43.2 }
    }
}
R2DomeBarePanels = group {
    object R2DomePanel(118.5, 150.5, 33.35, 36.5)
    difference {
        object R2DomePanel(151.6, 180.5, 33.35, 36.5)
        object R2DomeCut(154.2, 178, 33.75, 36.15)
    }
    object R2DomePanel(106.5, 113.3, 33.7, 35.65)
}
R2DomeCuts = group {
    object R2DomeCut(-6.5, 17.8, 33.35, 35.8)
    object R2DomeCut(-14.5, -8, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(-31, -16, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(-40, -32.5, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(-93, -42, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(-153.5, -97, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(-159.5, -155, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(151.6, 180.5, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(118.5, 150.5, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(104.5, 115.3, 33.35, 36)
    object R2DomeCut(108, 115.3, 36.3, 37.1)
    object R2DomeCut(80, 103, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(67, 78.3, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(54.5, 65, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(39, 52, 33.35, 36.5)
    object R2DomeCut(20.5, 26.5, 38.3, 40.4)
    object R2DomeCut(20.5, 26, 36, 37.9)
    for k in [0, 5] {
        object R2DomeCut(k * 60, k * 60 + 60, 40.9, 42.5, 0.175)
    }
    cylinder { min Y -0.45  max Y 0.2  scale [1.7, 1, 1.7]  rotate X R2EyeLean - 90  translate R2EyeAt }
    intersection {
        difference {
            sphere { scale R2DomeRadius + 0.2  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
            sphere { scale R2DomeRadius - 0.06  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
        }
        cube { scale [3.2, 0.6, 3.2]  translate Y 43.25 }
    }
}

// The fixtures on the dome are each built flat, in a frame of their own touching the dome at one point: +X
// toward his left, +Y up the dome and -Z straight out of it.  `R2OnDome` is how far to tip such a frame and
// how high to set it so that its origin lands on the dome `h` up, at `az` degrees round from straight ahead;
// a fixture's group ends `translate Z -R2DomeRadius  rotate X <tip>  translate Y <base>  rotate Y -az`.

// A logic display, `which` telling one from another so each has its own lamps: a white bezel 1.9 across and
// 1.32 tall standing 0.12 proud of the dome, with a dark window 1.42 by 0.9 let 0.08 into it, and in the
// window five rows of nine lamps, a third of them off and the rest blue or white, scattered.  A photograph
// of a fan-made droid from in front places the two on his front, one over the other in the blue frame to his
// right of the radar eye, and one of the finished droid from his front and right shows their white bezels.
primitive R2LogicDisplay(az, h, which) -> group {
    tip = toDegrees(asin(max(h - R2DomeMiddle, 0) / R2DomeRadius))
    base = min(h, R2DomeMiddle)
    return group {
        difference {
            cube { scale [0.95, 0.66, 0.26]  translate Z 0.14 }
            cube { scale [0.71, 0.45, 0.1]  translate Z -0.14  material R2LampOff }
            material R2White
        }
        for col in [0, 8] {
            for row in [0, 4] {
                if (random(which * 10 + col, row) < 0.35) {
                    cylinder {
                        min Y -0.06  max Y -0.04  scale [0.05, 1, 0.05]  rotate X 90
                        translate [(col - 4) * 0.155, (row - 2) * 0.17, 0]  material R2LampOff
                    }
                }
                else {
                    if (random(which * 10 + col, row, 7) < 0.5) {
                        cylinder {
                            min Y -0.06  max Y -0.04  scale [0.05, 1, 0.05]  rotate X 90
                            translate [(col - 4) * 0.155, (row - 2) * 0.17, 0]  material R2LampBlue
                        }
                    }
                    else {
                        cylinder {
                            min Y -0.06  max Y -0.04  scale [0.05, 1, 0.05]  rotate X 90
                            translate [(col - 4) * 0.155, (row - 2) * 0.17, 0]  material R2LampWhite
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        translate Z -R2DomeRadius  rotate X tip  translate Y base  rotate Y -az
    }
}

// An indicator: a round lens `radius` across its half, domed out of the dome by nearly a fifth of that in a
// thin dark ring, wearing `lens`.  The photograph from in front puts the front one, 1.6 across, in its blue
// panel, a little to his left of the panel's middle, and the fan-made droid lights it blue, as the front one
// is on screen; the photographs from behind put the rear one, 1.9 across, in the blue panel in the bare
// frame just to his left of straight behind, lit green.
primitive R2Indicator(az, h, lens, radius = 0.8) -> group {
    tip = toDegrees(asin(max(h - R2DomeMiddle, 0) / R2DomeRadius))
    base = min(h, R2DomeMiddle)
    return group {
        intersection {
            sphere { scale 2.7604 * radius  translate Z 2.5729 * radius }
            cylinder { min Y -0.2  max Y 0.4  scale [radius, 1, radius]  rotate X 90 }
            material lens
        }
        difference {
            cylinder { min Y -0.05  max Y 0.4  scale [radius + 0.1, 1, radius + 0.1]  rotate X 90 }
            cylinder { min Y -0.1  max Y 0.5  scale [radius, 1, radius]  rotate X 90 }
            material R2Dark
        }
        translate Z -R2DomeRadius  rotate X tip  translate Y base  rotate Y -az
    }
}

// A button: a blue dome 0.7 across standing 0.25 out of the dome.  The photographs from behind put two over
// the rear indicator.
primitive R2Button(az, h) -> group {
    tip = toDegrees(asin(max(h - R2DomeMiddle, 0) / R2DomeRadius))
    base = min(h, R2DomeMiddle)
    return group {
        sphere { scale 0.45  translate Z 0.2  material R2Blue }
        translate Z -R2DomeRadius  rotate X tip  translate Y base  rotate Y -az
    }
}

// A holoprojector: a knurled barrel 1.64 across on a ball it turns in, the ball sunk in the dome and standing
// 0.45 out of it and the barrel reaching 1 out, its open end dark with a lens down in it.  `yaw` turns the
// barrel round the dome the way `az` grows, and `pitch` up, from straight out of the dome; the ball wears
// `ball`.  The photograph from in front puts the front one on the bare dome to his left of the indicator,
// pointing out and turned forward, a little down, its ball silver as the finished droid's is (the fan-made
// droid's is blue); those from behind put the rear one on the bare dome just to his right of straight
// behind, pointing straight back, its ball silver, and the top one on the pie panel over his left shoulder,
// pointing up and back, its ball blue.
primitive R2Holoprojector(az, h, yaw, pitch, ball) -> group {
    tip = toDegrees(asin(max(h - R2DomeMiddle, 0) / R2DomeRadius))
    base = min(h, R2DomeMiddle)
    return group {
        sphere { scale 1.45  translate Z 1  material ball }
        group {
            difference {
                cylinder { min Y 0.6  max Y 2  scale [0.82, 1, 0.82] }
                cylinder { min Y 1.6  max Y 2.1  scale [0.66, 1, 0.66]  material R2Dark }
            }
            for k in [0, 23] {
                cube { scale [0.035, 0.32, 0.045]  translate [0.82, 1.6, 0]  rotate Y k * 15 }
            }
            cylinder { min Y 1.95  max Y 2  scale [0.84, 1, 0.84]  open }
            intersection {
                sphere { scale 0.9  translate Y 0.85 }
                cylinder { min Y 1.5  max Y 1.7  scale [0.66, 1, 0.66] }
                material R2EyeLens
            }
            material R2Metal
            rotate X -90  rotate X pitch  rotate Y -yaw  translate Z 1
        }
        translate Z -R2DomeRadius  rotate X tip  translate Y base  rotate Y -az
    }
}

// The rear logic display, in the long blue panel round from behind on his right: a blue housing 0.12 proud
// following the dome, from -152 to -118 round and 34.2 to 36.6 up, with a dark window let into it, -149.4 to
// -122.8 and 34.8 to 36, and in the window four rows of twenty lamps, scattered green and off.  The
// photographs from behind give the window, lit green, and the housing round it.
primitive R2RearLamp(col, row) -> group {
    az = -148.7 + col * 1.31
    h = 35.06 + row * 0.29
    tip = toDegrees(asin(max(h - R2DomeMiddle, 0) / R2DomeRadius))
    base = min(h, R2DomeMiddle)
    return group {
        if (random(30 + col, row) < 0.4) {
            cylinder { min Y -0.08  max Y -0.04  scale [0.07, 1, 0.07]  rotate X 90  material R2LampOff }
        }
        else {
            cylinder { min Y -0.08  max Y -0.04  scale [0.07, 1, 0.07]  rotate X 90  material R2LampGreen }
        }
        translate Z -R2DomeRadius  rotate X tip  translate Y base  rotate Y -az
    }
}
R2RearLogic = group {
    difference {
        object R2DomeRegion(-152, -118, 34.2, 36.6, 0, R2DomeRadius - 0.05, R2DomeRadius + 0.12)
        object R2DomeRegion(-149.4, -122.8, 34.8, 36, 0, R2DomeRadius + 0.04, R2DomeRadius + 0.3) {
            material R2LampOff
        }
        material R2Blue
    }
    for col in [0, 19] {
        for row in [0, 3] {
            object R2RearLamp(col, row)
        }
    }
}

// The fixtures on the dome's back and top, as the photographs from behind place them.
R2DomeBack = group {
    object R2Indicator(162.5, 35, R2IndicatorGreen, 0.95)
    object R2Button(164.4, 37.1)
    object R2Button(172.1, 37.1)
    object R2Holoprojector(-167.9, 35.3, -12, 0, R2Aluminum)
    object R2Holoprojector(150, 41.7, 0, 30, R2Blue)
    object R2RearLogic
}

// The fixtures on the dome's front, as the photograph from in front places them.
R2DomeFront = group {
    object R2LogicDisplay(-23.2, 35.96, 1)
    object R2LogicDisplay(-23.75, 34.4, 2)
    object R2Indicator(8, 34.46, R2IndicatorBlue)
    object R2Holoprojector(27.5, 35.2, -14, -14, R2Aluminum)
}

// The radar eye: a blue housing on the front of the dome, its flat top level at 40.4 and its bottom at 36.5,
// its sides on the meridians -14 and 19.5, and its face leaning back 30 degrees, standing about 0.7 out from
// the dome all the way up, with the lens let into it -- a recess 3.4 across and 0.45 deep, square to the
// face, and in it a black lens bulging to 0.1 short of the face -- a little to his right of the face's
// middle, on the dome's centerline.  A slot runs across the face's foot, and two grooves along each side,
// 0.3 and 0.6 behind the face.  A photograph of a fan-made droid from in front gives the face, the lens and
// the slot, and one from his left the lean, how far it stands out and the grooves; the 1977 side elevation
// leans it a little less.  The recess is cut from the dome too, where the dome comes up behind the face.
primitive R2EyeSideGroove(behind) -> group {
    return group {
        difference {
            cube { scale [6, 4, 0.03]  translate Z behind + 0.03  rotate X R2EyeLean  translate R2EyeAt }
            intersection {
                cube { scale 12  translate [12, 38, 0]  rotate Y 13.6 }
                cube { scale 12  translate [-12, 38, 0]  rotate Y -19.1 }
            }
        }
        material R2Dark
    }
}
R2RadarEye = group {
    difference {
        intersection {
            cube { scale [6, 1.95, 3.5]  translate [0, 38.45, -7] }
            cube { scale 8  translate Z 8  rotate X R2EyeLean  translate R2EyeAt }
            cube { scale 12  translate [12, 38, 0]  rotate Y 14 }
            cube { scale 12  translate [-12, 38, 0]  rotate Y -19.5 }
        }
        cylinder {
            min Y -0.45  max Y 0.2  scale [1.7, 1, 1.7]  rotate X R2EyeLean - 90  translate R2EyeAt
        }
        cube {
            scale [4, 0.06, 0.12]  translate Y -2.14  rotate X R2EyeLean  translate R2EyeAt  material R2Dark
        }
        object R2EyeSideGroove(0.3)
        object R2EyeSideGroove(0.6)
    }
    intersection {
        sphere { scale 4.3  translate Y -4.4 }
        cylinder { min Y -0.5  max Y 0  scale [1.7, 1, 1.7] }
        material R2EyeLens
        rotate X R2EyeLean - 90
        translate R2EyeAt
    }
    material R2Blue
}

// The dome: an aluminum hemisphere on an upright band as wide as the body, from just over the body's top --
// a dark gap 0.1 tall lets it turn -- with its panels let into it, a blue stripe painted round the band
// from 32.5 to 33.1, as the front elevation draws it and the photographs show it, and the radar eye.
R2Dome = group {
    cylinder { min Y R2DomeBase - 0.1  max Y R2DomeBase  scale [9, 1, 9]  material R2Dark }
    difference {
        union {
            intersection {
                sphere { scale R2DomeRadius  translate Y R2DomeMiddle }
                cube { scale [10, 5, 10]  translate Y R2DomeMiddle + 5 }
            }
            cylinder { min Y R2DomeBase  max Y R2DomeMiddle  scale [R2DomeRadius, 1, R2DomeRadius] }
        }
        object R2DomeCuts { material R2Dark }
    }
    group {
        object R2DomeBluePanels
        cylinder { min Y 32.5  max Y 33.1  open  scale [R2DomeRadius + 0.002, 1, R2DomeRadius + 0.002] }
        material R2Blue
    }
    object R2DomeBarePanels
    object R2RadarEye
    object R2DomeFront
    object R2DomeBack
    material R2Aluminum
}

// -- The body ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How far up the bottom of the skirt is, which the center leg hangs from.
R2SkirtBottom = 7.95

// The body's radius, and a piece of its skin between the radii `inner` and `outer`: between the meridians
// `from` and `to` -- degrees round from straight ahead, toward his left as they grow, less than half a turn
// apart -- and between the heights `low` and `high`.  The last box only holds the piece tight.
R2BodyRadius = 9.125
primitive R2BodyRegion(from, to, low, high, inner, outer) -> group {
    middle = (from + to) / 2
    half = (to - from) / 2
    across = outer * sin(min(half, 90) degrees) + 0.2
    near = inner * cos(min(half, 90) degrees) - 0.2
    return group {
        intersection {
            difference {
                cylinder { min Y low  max Y high  scale [outer, 1, outer] }
                cylinder { min Y low - 0.1  max Y high + 0.1  scale [inner, 1, inner] }
            }
            cube { scale 40  translate [40, 20, 0]  rotate Y -from }
            cube { scale 40  translate [-40, 20, 0]  rotate Y -to }
            cube {
                scale [across, (high - low) / 2 + 0.1, (outer + 0.2 - near) / 2]
                translate [0, (high + low) / 2, -(outer + 0.2 + near) / 2]
                rotate Y -middle
            }
        }
    }
}

// A panel on the body, to be cut from it: a groove round its edge 0.08 wide and 0.06 deep -- four strips, so
// that a ray meeting the panel's middle meets none of them -- and if `inset` is one, a plate inside that sunk
// 0.04, its edges 0.3 in from the groove and its corners rounded 0.35 -- the plate cut square to the dome's
// middle at the panel's middle, which on the barrel's curve is near enough.
primitive R2BodyPanel(from, to, low, high, inset = 0) -> group {
    r = R2BodyRadius
    d = toDegrees(0.04 / r)
    m = toDegrees(0.34 / r)
    half = r * sin(((to - from) / 2 - m) degrees)
    top = high - 0.34
    bottom = low + 0.34
    middle = (from + to) / 2
    near = r * cos(((to - from) / 2) degrees) - 0.5
    x = half - 0.35
    up = top - 0.35
    down = bottom + 0.35
    return group {
        group {
            object R2BodyRegion(from - d, to + d, high - 0.04, high + 0.04, r - 0.06, r + 0.3)
            object R2BodyRegion(from - d, to + d, low - 0.04, low + 0.04, r - 0.06, r + 0.3)
            object R2BodyRegion(from - d, from + d, low + 0.0405, high - 0.0405, r - 0.06, r + 0.3)
            object R2BodyRegion(to - d, to + d, low + 0.0405, high - 0.0405, r - 0.06, r + 0.3)
            material R2Gray
        }
        if (inset == 1) {
            intersection {
                difference {
                    cylinder { min Y bottom - 0.1  max Y top + 0.1  scale [r + 0.3, 1, r + 0.3] }
                    cylinder { min Y bottom - 0.2  max Y top + 0.2  scale [r - 0.04, 1, r - 0.04] }
                }
                union {
                    cube { scale [half, (top - bottom) / 2 - 0.35, r]  translate Y (top + bottom) / 2 }
                    cube { scale [x, (top - bottom) / 2, r]  translate Y (top + bottom) / 2 }
                    cylinder { min Y -r  max Y r  scale [0.35, 1, 0.35]  rotate X 90  translate [x, up, 0] }
                    cylinder { min Y -r  max Y r  scale [0.35, 1, 0.35]  rotate X 90  translate [-x, up, 0] }
                    cylinder { min Y -r  max Y r  scale [0.35, 1, 0.35]  rotate X 90  translate [x, down, 0] }
                    cylinder { min Y -r  max Y r  scale [0.35, 1, 0.35]  rotate X 90  translate [-x, down, 0] }
                }
                cube {
                    scale [half + 0.1, (top - bottom) / 2 + 0.1, (r + 0.4 - near) / 2]
                    translate [0, (top + bottom) / 2, -(r + 0.4 + near) / 2]
                }
                material R2White
                rotate Y -middle
            }
        }
    }
}

// Where the body's panels are, each as (from, to, low, high, inset).  Photographs of a fan-made droid from in
// front and from behind, each registered by fitting the camera that took it to the dome, place them; their
// heights are taken against the body's own top and the barrel's bottom in each, which the photographs put an
// inch and a half lower and three quarters of an inch higher respectively than the dome fit alone would.  The
// body's front is the middle of the blue vent panel, which the front elevation also centers, and its back the
// middle of the panel under the wide one.  The sides, under the shoulders, are not yet laid out.
R2BodyPanels = group {
    // The top panel on his front, holding the utility arms, and the slot's panel at its top.
    object R2BodyPanel(-42, 42, 25.05, 31.25, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(-33.2, 33.2, 30.17, 31.07, 0)
    // The tall panels either side of it.
    object R2BodyPanel(-66.5, -46.2, 16.9, 31.2, 1)
    object R2BodyPanel(46.2, 66.5, 16.9, 31.2, 1)
    // Under the top panel, to his right: a square one, the coin slots' and a narrow one.
    object R2BodyPanel(-41.9, -16.6, 22.3, 25.16, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(-44.1, -29.8, 16.69, 22.24, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(-28.6, -16.2, 16.69, 21.76, 1)
    // The blue vent panel in the middle, and the data panel to his left.
    object R2BodyPanel(-12.2, 12.2, 16.73, 24.95, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(15.7, 41.9, 16.8, 25.15, 1)
    // A row of four under those.
    object R2BodyPanel(-33.6, -19.9, 14.83, 16.22, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(-13.2, 12.5, 14.83, 16.22, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(19.3, 42.6, 14.83, 16.22, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(46.4, 68.9, 14.83, 16.22, 0)
    // Three narrow ones at the bottom, to his left.
    object R2BodyPanel(15.3, 27.9, 11.02, 14.48, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(31.4, 38.0, 11.02, 14.48, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(42.1, 49.4, 11.02, 14.48, 0)
    // The wide panel across his back, and a second groove inside it.
    object R2BodyPanel(140.3, 219.7, 25.05, 31.12, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(144.2, 215.8, 25.62, 30.42, 1)
    // The tall panels either side of it.
    object R2BodyPanel(112.5, 135.2, 16.9, 30.75, 1)
    object R2BodyPanel(224.8, 247.5, 16.9, 30.75, 1)
    // Three under it.
    object R2BodyPanel(143.1, 165.8, 16.71, 24.32, 1)
    object R2BodyPanel(170.4, 189.6, 17.13, 24.39, 1)
    object R2BodyPanel(194.2, 216.9, 16.71, 24.32, 1)
    // And a row of three under those.
    object R2BodyPanel(143.7, 165.4, 14.6, 16.45, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(170.5, 189.5, 15.13, 16.47, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(194.6, 216.3, 14.6, 16.45, 0)
}

// A vent in the body, between the meridians `from` and `to` and the heights `low` and `high`: a pocket 0.35
// deep, gray inside as the legs' grooves are, with `slats` blue louvers standing upright in it, 0.08 back
// from the barrel's face and filling a little over half its width, and a gray frame 0.12 wide standing 0.06
// proud round its mouth.
// `R2VentPocket` is the pocket, to be cut from the body; `R2Vent` the louvers and the frame.
primitive R2VentPocket(from, to, low, high) -> group {
    return group {
        object R2BodyRegion(from, to, low, high, R2BodyRadius - 0.35, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
        material R2Gray
    }
}
primitive R2Vent(from, to, low, high, slats) -> group {
    r = R2BodyRadius
    d = toDegrees(0.12 / r)
    pitch = (to - from) / slats
    return group {
        difference {
            object R2BodyRegion(from - d, to + d, low - 0.12, high + 0.12, r - 0.05, r + 0.06)
            object R2BodyRegion(from, to, low, high, r - 0.1, r + 0.2)
            material R2Gray
        }
        for k in [0, slats - 1] {
            object R2BodyRegion(
                from + (k + 0.22) * pitch, from + (k + 0.78) * pitch, low + 0.05, high - 0.05, r - 0.3, r - 0.08
            )
        }
        material R2Blue
    }
}

// The foot of a vent's frame, carried on down under its louvers to `low`: the frame's sides and bottom, 0.12
// wide, round a recess of its own 0.3 deep, gray as the vent's inside is.  `R2VentFootPocket` is the recess,
// to be cut from the body.
primitive R2VentFootPocket(from, to, low, high) -> group {
    return group {
        object R2BodyRegion(from, to, low + 0.12, high - 0.12, R2BodyRadius - 0.3, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
        material R2Gray
    }
}
primitive R2VentFoot(from, to, low, high) -> group {
    r = R2BodyRadius
    d = toDegrees(0.12 / r)
    return group {
        difference {
            object R2BodyRegion(from - d, to + d, low, high - 0.12, r - 0.05, r + 0.06)
            object R2BodyRegion(from, to, low + 0.12, high, r - 0.1, r + 0.2)
        }
        material R2Gray
    }
}

// The vents: one on each side under the shoulder, louvered five times and half hidden by the leg -- a
// photograph of a fan-made droid from his left and one of the finished droid from his front and right show
// them, a little forward of the leg's shank, from about the coin slots' foot up to the horseshoe's lower
// fittings -- and two low ones on opposite corners of the barrel, one to his right in front, which the
// photograph from in front places, and one to his left behind, which the one from behind does.  The front one's
// frame carries on down below its louvers to the foot of the coin return's plate beside it, round a recess of
// its own, as a photograph of the finished droid from his front and right shows it.
R2BodyVentPockets = group {
    object R2VentPocket(74, 100, 17, 23)
    object R2VentPocket(-100, -74, 17, 23)
    object R2VentPocket(-76, -50.4, 12.5, 16.5)
    object R2VentFootPocket(-76, -50.4, 11.18, 12.5)
    object R2VentPocket(108, 137, 10.8, 17.4)
}
R2BodyVents = group {
    object R2Vent(74, 100, 17, 23, 5)
    object R2Vent(-100, -74, 17, 23, 5)
    object R2Vent(-76, -50.4, 12.5, 16.5, 5)
    object R2VentFoot(-76, -50.4, 11.18, 12.5)
    object R2Vent(108, 137, 10.8, 17.4, 5)
}

// The utility arms in the top panel on his front, and the slot over them, as the front elevation draws them.
// Each arm is seen from in front as a long flat blade: a thick end, a handle 1.44 tall, a thinner neck, and a
// tip that swells to near the handle's height before tapering to its end -- the same shape above and below
// its middle line, and the lower arm the upper one turned end for end, the upper's tip to his right and the
// lower's to his left.  The outline from straight in front is carried back into the barrel: the arm is the
// outline between the barrel's radius less 1.6 and less 0.3, so its face is set back a little and follows
// the barrel's curve -- but for its neck, which dips into the body behind the tip, below -- and it stands in
// a pocket of the same outline 0.07 wider all round, dark inside, which is the black line round each arm in
// the photographs.  A photograph of a fan-made droid from his left shows the arms set back in their pockets.
// (The last box in each piece only holds it tight.)  The slot over them is a dark opening 0.45 tall in a
// panel just under the dome, with a blue lip along its top, as the photographs show it, and a groove runs
// across the top panel between the arms.
R2UpperArmFront = path {
    move to -4.940, 29.380
    line to -4.740, 29.380
    line to -3.840, 29.570
    line to -2.870, 29.600
    line to -2.490, 29.300
    line to -0.970, 29.260
    line to -0.970, 29.620
    line to 3.380, 29.620
    line to 3.880, 29.390
    line to 4.950, 29.390
    line to 4.950, 28.410
    line to 3.880, 28.410
    line to 3.380, 28.180
    line to -0.970, 28.180
    line to -0.970, 28.540
    line to -2.490, 28.500
    line to -2.870, 28.200
    line to -3.840, 28.230
    line to -4.740, 28.420
    line to -4.940, 28.420
    close
}
R2UpperArmPocket = path {
    move to -5.010, 29.450
    line to -4.747, 29.450
    line to -3.848, 29.640
    line to -2.847, 29.671
    line to -2.465, 29.369
    line to -1.040, 29.332
    line to -1.040, 29.690
    line to 3.395, 29.690
    line to 3.895, 29.460
    line to 5.020, 29.460
    line to 5.020, 28.340
    line to 3.895, 28.340
    line to 3.395, 28.110
    line to -1.040, 28.110
    line to -1.040, 28.468
    line to -2.465, 28.431
    line to -2.847, 28.129
    line to -3.848, 28.160
    line to -4.747, 28.350
    line to -5.010, 28.350
    close
}
R2LowerArmFront = path {
    move to 4.950, 26.970
    line to 4.750, 26.970
    line to 3.850, 27.160
    line to 2.880, 27.190
    line to 2.500, 26.890
    line to 0.980, 26.850
    line to 0.980, 27.210
    line to -3.370, 27.210
    line to -3.870, 26.980
    line to -4.940, 26.980
    line to -4.940, 26.000
    line to -3.870, 26.000
    line to -3.370, 25.770
    line to 0.980, 25.770
    line to 0.980, 26.130
    line to 2.500, 26.090
    line to 2.880, 25.790
    line to 3.850, 25.820
    line to 4.750, 26.010
    line to 4.950, 26.010
    close
}
R2LowerArmPocket = path {
    move to 5.020, 27.040
    line to 4.757, 27.040
    line to 3.858, 27.230
    line to 2.857, 27.261
    line to 2.475, 26.959
    line to 1.050, 26.922
    line to 1.050, 27.280
    line to -3.385, 27.280
    line to -3.885, 27.050
    line to -5.010, 27.050
    line to -5.010, 25.930
    line to -3.885, 25.930
    line to -3.385, 25.700
    line to 1.050, 25.700
    line to 1.050, 26.058
    line to 2.475, 26.021
    line to 2.857, 25.719
    line to 3.858, 25.750
    line to 4.757, 25.940
    line to 5.020, 25.940
    close
}

primitive R2ArmShell(inner, outer) -> group {
    return group {
        difference {
            cylinder { min Y 25  max Y 31.4  scale [outer, 1, outer] }
            cylinder { min Y 24.9  max Y 31.5  scale [inner, 1, inner] }
        }
    }
}
// The bend where an arm's tip meets its neck, in a frame of its own: the joint straight ahead on the arm's
// face, 8.825 from the barrel's axis, and the neck running toward +X.  The tip turns square into the body
// there, 0.8 deep, round a tight bend -- its outside 0.15 round, its inside 0.12 -- and the neck rises back
// out at 23 degrees to meet the handle on the face where the neck ends, 10 degrees round.  `R2ArmBendCut` is
// what is taken from the arm, `R2ArmBendFill` the inside of the bend put back.
R2ArmBendCut = group {
    intersection {
        cube { scale [4, 1.2, 4]  translate [4, 0, -4]  rotate Y 23.26  translate Z -8.025 }
        cube { scale [4, 1.2, 4]  translate [4, 0, -8] }
    }
    difference {
        cube { scale [0.075, 1.2, 0.225]  translate [-0.075, 0, -8.9] }
        cylinder { min Y -1.3  max Y 1.3  scale [0.15, 1, 0.15]  translate [-0.15, 0, -8.675] }
    }
}
R2ArmBendFill = difference {
    intersection {
        cube { scale [4, 1.2, 4]  translate [4, 0, -4]  rotate Y 23.26  translate Z -8.025 }
        cube { scale [0.0835, 1.2, 0.091]  translate [0.0835, 0, -8.116] }
    }
    cylinder { min Y -1.3  max Y 1.3  scale [0.12, 1, 0.12]  translate [0.12, 0, -8.207] }
}

R2ArmPockets = group {
    intersection {
        extrusion { path R2UpperArmPocket  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90 }
        object R2ArmShell(R2BodyRadius - 1.7, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
        cube { scale [5.2, 0.85, 1.7]  translate [0, 28.9, -7.8] }
    }
    intersection {
        extrusion { path R2LowerArmPocket  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90 }
        object R2ArmShell(R2BodyRadius - 1.7, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
        cube { scale [5.2, 0.85, 1.7]  translate [0, 26.49, -7.8] }
    }
    object R2BodyRegion(-30.3, 30.3, 30.45, 30.9, R2BodyRadius - 1.2, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
    material R2Hole
}
R2UtilityArms = group {
    intersection {
        extrusion { path R2UpperArmFront  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90 }
        cube { scale [5.2, 0.85, 1.7]  translate [0, 28.9, -7.8] }
        union {
            difference {
                object R2ArmShell(R2BodyRadius - 1.6, R2BodyRadius - 0.3)
                object R2ArmBendCut { rotate Y 16.39  translate Y 28.9 }
            }
            object R2ArmBendFill { rotate Y 16.39  translate Y 28.9 }
        }
    }
    intersection {
        extrusion { path R2LowerArmFront  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90 }
        cube { scale [5.2, 0.85, 1.7]  translate [0, 26.49, -7.8] }
        union {
            difference {
                object R2ArmShell(R2BodyRadius - 1.6, R2BodyRadius - 0.3)
                object R2ArmBendCut { scale [-1, 1, 1]  rotate Y -16.46  translate Y 26.49 }
            }
            object R2ArmBendFill { scale [-1, 1, 1]  rotate Y -16.46  translate Y 26.49 }
        }
    }
    object R2BodyRegion(-30.1, 30.1, 30.76, 30.9, R2BodyRadius - 1.1, R2BodyRadius - 0.12)
    material R2Blue
}
R2ArmGrooves = group {
    object R2BodyRegion(-42, 42, 27.82, 27.9, R2BodyRadius - 0.06, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
    material R2Gray
}

// A rounded rectangle carried straight back from in front, through the front of the barrel: `hw` and `hh`
// across and up from its middle at height `cy`, its corners rounded `r`.  Cut with a shell, it gives a shape
// that follows the barrel's curve.
primitive R2RoundedPrism(cy, hw, hh, r) -> group {
    return group {
        union {
            cube { scale [hw, hh - r, 6]  translate [0, cy, -6] }
            cube { scale [hw - r, hh, 6]  translate [0, cy, -6] }
            cylinder { min Y 0  max Y 12  scale [r, 1, r]  rotate X -90  translate [hw - r, cy + hh - r, 0] }
            cylinder { min Y 0  max Y 12  scale [r, 1, r]  rotate X -90  translate [r - hw, cy + hh - r, 0] }
            cylinder { min Y 0  max Y 12  scale [r, 1, r]  rotate X -90  translate [hw - r, cy - hh + r, 0] }
            cylinder { min Y 0  max Y 12  scale [r, 1, r]  rotate X -90  translate [r - hw, cy - hh + r, 0] }
        }
    }
}

// The blue vent panel in the middle of his front: the panel painted blue, and in it two vents one over the
// other, each a rounded opening 2.9 across and 3.2 tall, its corners rounded 0.9, in a bright rim 0.21 wide
// standing 0.12 proud, dark inside, with four slats across it, tilted down to the front, set back 0.12 from
// the face.  A photograph of a fan-made droid from in front places them; one of the finished droid from his
// front and right, and the front elevation, show both with slats across.  `R2VentOpening` is the opening, to
// be cut from the body.
R2VentPanelBlue = group {
    object R2BodyRegion(-11.95, 11.95, 16.77, 24.91, R2BodyRadius - 0.1, R2BodyRadius + 0.003)
    material R2Blue
}
primitive R2VentOpening(cy) -> group {
    return group {
        intersection {
            object R2RoundedPrism(cy, 1.44, 1.58, 0.9)
            object R2BodyRegion(-15, 15, cy - 1.7, cy + 1.7, R2BodyRadius - 0.7, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
        }
        material R2Hole
    }
}
primitive R2VentRim(cy) -> group {
    return group {
        intersection {
            difference {
                object R2RoundedPrism(cy, 1.65, 1.84, 1.1)
                object R2RoundedPrism(cy, 1.44, 1.58, 0.9)
            }
            object R2BodyRegion(-15, 15, cy - 1.9, cy + 1.9, R2BodyRadius - 0.2, R2BodyRadius + 0.12)
        }
        intersection {
            object R2RoundedPrism(cy, 1.44, 1.58, 0.9)
            group {
                for k in [0, 3] {
                    cube {
                        scale [1.5, 0.035, 0.25]  rotate X -25
                        translate [0, cy - 1.14 + k * 0.76, -R2BodyRadius + 0.37]
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        // The slats are the rim's satin: tilted down to the front, a polished slat mirrors an eye above it up
        // into the slat over it or the dark inside, and the vent came out black.
        material R2Satin
    }
}

// A coin slot, `top` its top and `az` the middle of its face: a bright wedge 1.24 across standing 0.4 out of
// the body, flat on top, with an upright face 0.28 tall and an underside sloping back to the body 0.5 under
// its top, and a dark slot 1 across and 0.08 tall in its face.  Its side is drawn front to the right, as the
// legs' are, and turned to stand out of the barrel at `az`.
primitive R2CoinSlot(az, top) -> group {
    r = R2BodyRadius
    return group {
        difference {
            extrusion {
                path R2CoinSlotSide  min Y -0.62  max Y 0.62  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90
            }
            cube { scale [0.5, 0.04, 0.15]  translate [0, -0.13, -r - 0.4]  material R2Hole }
        }
        material R2Satin
        translate Y top
        rotate Y -az
    }
}
R2CoinSlotSide = path {
    move to 8.825, 0
    line to 9.525, 0
    line to 9.525, -0.28
    line to 9.125, -0.5
    line to 8.825, -0.5
    close
}

// The six coin slots in their panel to his right of the vent panel, one over another 0.88 apart, as a
// photograph of a fan-made droid from in front places them; one of the finished droid from his front and
// right shows their wedges.
R2CoinSlots = group {
    for k in [0, 5] {
        object R2CoinSlot(-37.2, 21.84 - k * 0.88)
    }
}

// The data port on the panel to his left of the vents: a dark flange 2 across on the panel, a bright collar,
// and a barrel 1.44 across standing 1.1 out of the body, its end dark round a bright ring and a pin.  The
// photograph from in front places it, and one from his left shows its shape.
R2DataPort = group {
    cylinder { min Y 0  max Y 0.12  scale [1, 1, 1]  material R2Dark }
    cylinder { min Y 0.12  max Y 0.55  scale [0.78, 1, 0.78]  material R2Chrome }
    difference {
        cylinder { min Y 0.55  max Y 1.1  scale [0.72, 1, 0.72] }
        cylinder { min Y 0.85  max Y 1.2  scale [0.56, 1, 0.56]  material R2Hole }
    }
    difference {
        cylinder { min Y 0.85  max Y 1.02  scale [0.36, 1, 0.36] }
        cylinder { min Y 0.8  max Y 1.1  scale [0.28, 1, 0.28]  material R2Hole }
        material R2Chrome
    }
    cylinder { min Y 0.85  max Y 1.0  scale [0.1, 1, 0.1]  material R2Chrome }
    material R2Metal
    rotate X -90
    translate [0, 21.7, -R2BodyRadius + 0.02]
    rotate Y -27.8
}

// -- The lower row --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A coin return: a gray metal plate 0.04 proud between the meridians `from` and `to` and the heights `low`
// and `high`, with a square opening `hw` and `hh` across and up from its middle at `az` and `cy`, its corners
// rounded a little, going into the body as far as the return's face, the same gray: a face leaning back, 1
// in at the opening's foot and 2 at its top.  `R2CoinReturnPocket` is the opening, to be cut from the body;
// `R2CoinReturn` the plate.  Photographs of a fan-made droid from in front and from behind place one in
// front, its opening toward his right, and two behind, theirs in their middles; one of the finished droid
// from his front and right shows the front one's plate and its open box.
primitive R2CoinReturnOpening(az, cy, hw, hh) -> group {
    return group { object R2RoundedPrism(cy, hw, hh, 0.12) { rotate Y -az } }
}
primitive R2CoinReturnPocket(az, cy, hw, hh) -> group {
    return group {
        intersection {
            object R2CoinReturnOpening(az, cy, hw, hh)
            object R2BodyRegion(
                az - 20, az + 20, cy - hh - 0.1, cy + hh + 0.1, R2BodyRadius - 2.3, R2BodyRadius + 0.3
            )
            cube {
                scale 10  translate Z -10  rotate X toDegrees(atan(1 / (2 * hh)))
                translate [0, cy - hh, 1 - R2BodyRadius]  rotate Y -az
            }
        }
        material R2Metal
    }
}
primitive R2CoinReturn(from, to, low, high, az, cy, hw, hh) -> group {
    return group {
        difference {
            object R2BodyRegion(from, to, low, high, R2BodyRadius - 0.05, R2BodyRadius + 0.04)
            object R2CoinReturnOpening(az, cy, hw, hh) { material R2Metal }
        }
        material R2Metal
    }
}

// A power coupler, `az` round and `cy` up, in a frame of its own touching the body there: a round pocket 3.24
// across, 0.35 deep at its middle, in a blue plate, ringed in satin standing just proud.  On the front one a
// blue disc lies in the pocket under a dark cross with satin edges and a satin hub, as the photograph from in
// front shows it; on the back one, `back`, the pocket is a dark bowl with a small satin tab at its top, as the
// photographs from behind show it.  `R2CouplerPocket` is the pocket, to be cut from the body.
primitive R2CouplerPocket(az, cy) -> group {
    return group {
        cylinder { min Y -1  max Y 0.35  scale [1.62, 1, 1.62]  rotate X 90 }
        material R2Dark
        translate Z -R2BodyRadius  translate Y cy  rotate Y -az
    }
}
primitive R2Coupler(az, cy, back = 0) -> group {
    return group {
        difference {
            cylinder { min Y -0.05  max Y 0.35  scale [1.62, 1, 1.62]  rotate X 90 }
            cylinder { min Y -0.1  max Y 0.4  scale [1.47, 1, 1.47]  rotate X 90 }
        }
        if (back == 1) {
            cylinder { min Y 0.33  max Y 0.35  scale [1.47, 1, 1.47]  rotate X 90  material R2Hole }
            cube { scale [0.22, 0.1, 0.1]  translate [0, 1.2, 0.25] }
        }
        else {
            cylinder { min Y 0.29  max Y 0.35  scale [1.25, 1, 1.25]  rotate X 90  material R2Blue }
            cube { scale [0.43, 1.25, 0.065]  translate Z 0.285 }
            cube { scale [1.25, 0.43, 0.065]  translate Z 0.285 }
            cube { scale [0.35, 1.26, 0.075]  translate Z 0.275  material R2Dark }
            cube { scale [1.26, 0.35, 0.075]  translate Z 0.275  material R2Dark }
            cylinder { min Y 0.05  max Y 0.35  scale [0.38, 1, 0.38]  rotate X 90 }
        }
        material R2Satin
        translate Z -R2BodyRadius  translate Y cy  rotate Y -az
    }
}

// An octagonal port, `az` round and `cy` up: a dark pocket 2.9 across and 3.4 tall, its corners cut 0.6 and
// 0.45 deep, framed in gray 0.15 wide standing just proud, with a dark round lens in it 2 across.  The
// photographs from in front and from his left put one on his front left, low; those from behind and the 1977
// side elevation one on his back right.
R2OctagonFront = path {
    move to -0.85, -1.7
    line to 0.85, -1.7
    line to 1.45, -1.1
    line to 1.45, 1.1
    line to 0.85, 1.7
    line to -0.85, 1.7
    line to -1.45, 1.1
    line to -1.45, -1.1
    close
}
R2OctagonRim = path {
    move to -0.94, -1.85
    line to 0.94, -1.85
    line to 1.6, -1.19
    line to 1.6, 1.19
    line to 0.94, 1.85
    line to -0.94, 1.85
    line to -1.6, 1.19
    line to -1.6, -1.19
    close
}
primitive R2OctagonPocket(az, cy) -> group {
    return group {
        intersection {
            extrusion { path R2OctagonFront  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90  translate Y cy  rotate Y -az }
            object R2BodyRegion(az - 15, az + 15, cy - 1.8, cy + 1.8, R2BodyRadius - 0.45, R2BodyRadius + 0.3)
        }
        material R2Hole
    }
}
primitive R2OctagonPort(az, cy) -> group {
    return group {
        intersection {
            difference {
                extrusion { path R2OctagonRim  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90  translate Y cy  rotate Y -az }
                extrusion { path R2OctagonFront  min Y 0  max Y 12  rotate X -90  translate Y cy  rotate Y -az }
            }
            object R2BodyRegion(az - 15, az + 15, cy - 2, cy + 2, R2BodyRadius - 0.05, R2BodyRadius + 0.03)
            material R2Gray
        }
        group {
            cylinder { min Y 0.3  max Y 0.5  scale [1, 1, 1]  rotate X 90  material R2Dark }
            difference {
                cylinder { min Y 0.27  max Y 0.5  scale [1, 1, 1]  rotate X 90 }
                cylinder { min Y 0.2  max Y 0.55  scale [0.9, 1, 0.9]  rotate X 90 }
                material R2Metal
            }
            translate Z -R2BodyRadius  translate Y cy  rotate Y -az
        }
    }
}

// The lower row's places, front and back, as the photographs from in front and from behind place them: the
// coin return to his right in front, the power coupler in the middle and the octagonal port to his left; the
// two coin returns either side of the power coupler behind, and the octagonal port to his right.
R2LowerRowPaint = group {
    object R2BodyRegion(-10.6, 10.1, 11.05, 14.25, R2BodyRadius - 0.1, R2BodyRadius + 0.003)
    object R2BodyRegion(170.2, 190, 11.3, 14.6, R2BodyRadius - 0.1, R2BodyRadius + 0.003)
    material R2Blue
}
R2LowerRowPockets = group {
    object R2BodyPanel(-10.6, 10.1, 11.05, 14.25, 0)
    object R2BodyPanel(170.2, 190, 11.3, 14.6, 0)
    object R2CoinReturnPocket(-34.56, 12.59, 0.875, 1.23)
    object R2CoinReturnPocket(153.9, 12.85, 1.1, 1.15)
    object R2CoinReturnPocket(-153.9, 12.85, 1.1, 1.15)
    object R2CouplerPocket(-0.25, 12.62)
    object R2CouplerPocket(180.1, 13)
    object R2OctagonPocket(65.8, 13.25)
    object R2OctagonPocket(-125, 12.6)
}
R2LowerRow = group {
    object R2CoinReturn(-43.1, -15.5, 11.18, 14.44, -34.56, 12.59, 0.875, 1.23)
    object R2CoinReturn(142.2, 165.6, 11.3, 14.45, 153.9, 12.85, 1.1, 1.15)
    object R2CoinReturn(-165.2, -143.7, 11.3, 14.45, -153.9, 12.85, 1.1, 1.15)
    object R2Coupler(-0.25, 12.62)
    object R2Coupler(180.1, 13, 1)
    object R2OctagonPort(65.8, 13.25)
    object R2OctagonPort(-125, 12.6)
}

// -- The skirt ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// A strut on the skirt, `az` round: a flat bar 0.44 wide standing 0.15 out of the skirt, running straight down
// it from under the step at the barrel's foot to the skirt's bottom -- the skirt's own slope, 32.9 degrees
// from upright, 2.9 long, ending just inside both.  Built straight ahead and turned round to `az`.
primitive R2SkirtStrut(az) -> group {
    return group {
        cube { scale [0.22, 1.45, 0.1]  translate Z -0.05  rotate X -32.86  translate [0, 9.292, -7.697] }
        rotate Y -az
    }
}

// The skirt's struts, as photographs of a fan-made droid from in front and from behind place them: three either
// side of his middle in front, the two nearest 18 apart and the others in a pair over each foot, and two either
// side of his middle behind.  Under the legs the skirt is plain, as the one from his left shows it.
R2SkirtStruts = group {
    object R2SkirtStrut(-55)
    object R2SkirtStrut(-38.5)
    object R2SkirtStrut(-9)
    object R2SkirtStrut(9)
    object R2SkirtStrut(38.5)
    object R2SkirtStrut(55)
    object R2SkirtStrut(149)
    object R2SkirtStrut(172)
    object R2SkirtStrut(188)
    object R2SkirtStrut(211)
}

// The body: its outline turned about its axis, as the 1977 side elevation draws it, front and back alike --
// the barrel 18.25 across, straight down from 31.4 under the dome to 10.55; then a flat ring 0.6 wide
// stepping in from its edge; then the skirt, narrowing down and in to its flat bottom, 6.83 round at 7.95 --
// with its panels, vents and the arms' pockets cut into it, and the vents' louvers and frames and the arms.  The
// cuts are gathered into one group, so that they are one side of one difference, sorted among themselves.
R2Body = group {
    difference {
        union {
            lathe {
                path {
                    move to 0, R2SkirtBottom
                    line to 6.83, R2SkirtBottom
                    line to 8.51, 10.55
                    line to 9.125, 10.55
                    line to 9.125, 31.4
                    line to 0, 31.4
                }
            }
            object R2VentPanelBlue
            object R2LowerRowPaint
        }
        group {
            object R2BodyPanels
            object R2BodyVentPockets
            object R2ArmPockets
            object R2ArmGrooves
            object R2VentOpening(22.96)
            object R2VentOpening(18.86)
            object R2LowerRowPockets
        }
    }
    object R2BodyVents
    object R2UtilityArms
    object R2VentRim(22.96)
    object R2VentRim(18.86)
    object R2DataPort
    object R2CoinSlots
    object R2LowerRow
    object R2SkirtStruts
    material R2White
}

// -- The legs ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// How high the shoulder hubs stand, which the outer legs hang from and the body turns about: the legs are
// 31 tall, and their round tops are 8.75 across, centered on the hubs.
R2Shoulder = 26.625

// One outer leg's side view, front to the right: the round top, straight sides falling to a V that narrows
// it to its 4.5-in shank, and the shank widening again to the ankle block.  The top is a horseshoe, open
// in its middle round the hub on the body that the leg turns on: the opening is 3.2 across, round at the
// top about the pivot and falling 2 below it -- the second run here, which the extrusion cuts out.
R2LegProfile = path {
    move to -4.375, 26.625
    curve -4.375, 29.04, -2.42, 31 to 0, 31
    curve 2.42, 31, 4.375, 29.04 to 4.375, 26.625
    line to 4.375, 21.8
    line to 2.25, 20.6
    line to 2.25, 12.6
    line to 2.65, 12.6
    line to 2.65, 7.4
    line to -2.65, 7.4
    line to -2.65, 12.6
    line to -2.25, 12.6
    line to -2.25, 20.6
    line to -4.375, 21.8
    close

    move to -1.6, 24.625
    line to 1.6, 24.625
    line to 1.6, 26.625
    curve 1.6, 27.509, 0.884, 28.225 to 0, 28.225
    curve -0.884, 28.225, -1.6, 27.509 to -1.6, 26.625
    close
}

// The shank's face either side of the blue housing, sunk 0.93 -- a third of the leg's thickness -- from the
// top of the booster cover up to the horseshoe's underside, whose slant runs on in to meet the housing
// rather than stopping at the shank's edge: two runs, before and behind, with the same profile convention.
R2ShankSink = path {
    move to 1, 12.8
    line to 2.3, 12.8
    line to 2.3, 20.63
    line to 1, 19.89
    close

    move to -1, 12.8
    line to -1, 19.89
    line to -2.3, 20.63
    line to -2.3, 12.8
    close
}

// The groove round the horseshoe's edge, running parallel to the face a third of the leg's thickness back
// from it -- level with the sunken face below -- over the round top, down both sides and along the slant
// underneath to the shank, 0.08 deep: the band between the outline drawn out by 0.2 and drawn in by 0.08,
// to be cut 0.12 wide.  Front to the right, as the leg's profile.
R2HorseshoeGroove = path {
    move to -2.3, 20.4
    line to -4.575, 21.684
    line to -4.575, 26.625
    curve -4.575, 29.152, -2.527, 31.2 to 0, 31.2
    curve 2.527, 31.2, 4.575, 29.152 to 4.575, 26.625
    line to 4.575, 21.684
    line to 2.3, 20.4
    line to 2.3, 20.72
    line to 4.295, 21.847
    line to 4.295, 26.625
    curve 4.295, 28.997, 2.372, 30.92 to 0, 30.92
    curve -2.372, 30.92, -4.295, 28.997 to -4.295, 26.625
    line to -4.295, 21.847
    line to -2.3, 20.72
    close
}

// A foot's side view: a base 12.6 long under a top 7, with sloping toes and heels, above its sole.
R2FootProfile = path {
    move to -6.3, 0.8
    line to 6.3, 0.8
    line to 3.5, 5.7
    line to -3.5, 5.7
    close
}

// A foot's front view, its outer face to the right: 7.2 wide at the bottom, the outer face leaning in
// 1.5 by the top and the inner one upright.  A center foot leans in on both sides.
R2FootFront = path {
    move to -3.6, 0.8
    line to 3.6, 0.8
    line to 2.1, 5.7
    line to -3.6, 5.7
    close
}
R2FootFrontBoth = path {
    move to -3.6, 0.8
    line to 3.6, 0.8
    line to 2.1, 5.7
    line to -2.1, 5.7
    close
}

// The sole under a foot, 0.8 deep, its sides and ends sloping in by 0.25 to its bottom: an upside-down
// trapezoid seen from the side or from in front, as the five-view drawing and the photographs show it,
// with a row of dark slots round it, 0.2 tall and 0.1 deep -- seven 1.45 long down each side and four 1.35
// long across each end, as the photographs count them.
R2FootSoleSide = path {
    move to -6.3, 0.8
    line to 6.3, 0.8
    line to 6.05, 0
    line to -6.05, 0
    close
}
R2FootSoleFront = path {
    move to -3.6, 0.8
    line to 3.6, 0.8
    line to 3.35, 0
    line to -3.35, 0
    close
}
R2FootSoleSlots = group {
    for along in [-3, 3] {
        cube { scale [0.155, 0.1, 0.725]  translate [3.545, 0.45, along * 1.75] }
        cube { scale [0.155, 0.1, 0.725]  translate [-3.545, 0.45, along * 1.75] }
    }
    for across in [0, 3] {
        cube { scale [0.675, 0.1, 0.155]  translate [-2.625 + across * 1.75, 0.45, 6.245] }
        cube { scale [0.675, 0.1, 0.155]  translate [-2.625 + across * 1.75, 0.45, -6.245] }
    }
    material R2Dark
}

// The panel on a foot's leaning side, seen from the side: a rim round the face's edge, 0.27 wide along its
// top, 0.25 in from its sloping ends and 0.22 over its sole, rounded at its bottom corners, within which the
// face is sunk -- all but a band across its top and a pad in either lower corner, which stand at the face as
// the rim does, with the wheel's housing standing out between the pads.  A pad's outer edge runs with the
// foot's end 0.45 in from it, its inner edge round the housing 0.4 out from it, from the housing's foot 1.8
// up to 3.31.  The 1977 side elevation draws them all, on a foot longer than this one: the rim and the gaps
// are its own, and the band and the pads are drawn in to fit.  One run for the rim's inside and one for each
// thing standing within it, so that even-odd filling leaves only the sunken part.
R2FootField = path {
    move to -5.3212, 1.02
    line to 5.3212, 1.02
    curve 5.5899, 1.02, 5.7584, 1.3103 to 5.6251, 1.5436
    line to 3.4043, 5.43
    line to -3.4043, 5.43
    line to -5.6251, 1.5436
    curve -5.7584, 1.3103, -5.5899, 1.02 to -5.3212, 1.02
    close
    move to 3.6, 1.8
    line to 5.2786, 1.8
    line to 4.4157, 3.31
    line to 3.268, 3.31
    curve 3.4867, 2.8366, 3.6, 2.3214 to 3.6, 1.8
    close
    move to -3.6, 1.8
    curve -3.6, 2.3214, -3.4867, 2.8366 to -3.268, 3.31
    line to -4.4157, 3.31
    line to -5.2786, 1.8
    close
    move to -3.3271, 5.04
    line to 3.3271, 5.04
    line to 3.1671, 5.32
    line to -3.1671, 5.32
    close
}

// The cut that sinks it 0.08 into the face, measured square to the face: the panel seen from the side, out
// across the face, kept beyond a plane 0.08 inside it.  The face leans in 17.02 degrees.  In the foot's frame,
// for its +X side.
R2FootSideCut = intersection {
    extrusion { path R2FootField  min Y -3.7  max Y 0  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
    cube { scale [1, 7, 7]  translate X 1  rotate Z 17.0205  translate [3.5235, 0.7766, 0] }
    material R2White
}

// The block on a wheel's housing, standing 0.06 out of its face: 2.48 across, from the housing's foot up to
// its round, with two dark slots across it 2.1 long, 0.13 tall and 0.04 deep, 4.52 and 4.02 up.  The 1977
// side elevation draws the block and the slots.  In the foot's frame, for its +X side.
R2FootArchBlock = difference {
    intersection {
        cylinder { min Y 3.29  max Y 3.354  scale [3.2, 1, 3.2]  rotate Z -90  translate Y 1.8 }
        cube { scale [0.1, 1.6, 1.24]  translate [3.32, 3.4, 0] }
    }
    cube { scale [0.04, 0.065, 1.05]  translate [3.354, 4.52, 0]  material R2Dark }
    cube { scale [0.04, 0.065, 1.05]  translate [3.354, 4.02, 0]  material R2Dark }
    material R2White
}

// A foot, in its own frame with its outer face toward +X: the block its two views make, on its sole, with
// the half-round housing over its wheel standing out of its leaning face -- 3.2 round about an axle 1.8 up,
// its flat face upright and flush with the leaning face at its own bottom edge, so that it stands proud as
// the face leans away above it -- a block with two slots on the housing, and the face round it sunk within a
// rim but for a band and two pads (`R2FootField`).  `both` puts a leaning face and a housing on each side,
// as a center foot has.  Down its middle a slot 1.2 wide runs right through it from front to back, from the
// top down to 3.74, which the fin under its leg stands in on its pivot; and on its front and its back, under
// the slot, a plate as wide and 3.1 long lies on the sloping face, its bottom 1.05 up, which the hoses come
// into on an outer foot's front.  The front elevation and the photographs show the front plate and the slot
// over it; the five-view drawing of the finished droid shows the back one.  The ends slope 29.74 degrees.
// Front is toward -Z.
primitive R2Foot(both = 0) -> group {
    return group {
        if (both == 1) {
            difference {
                intersection {
                    extrusion { path R2FootProfile  min Y -3.7  max Y 3.7  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
                    extrusion { path R2FootFrontBoth  min Y -6.4  max Y 6.4  rotate X -90 }
                }
                cube { scale [0.6, 1.4, 7]  translate Y 5.14 }
                object R2FootSideCut
                object R2FootSideCut { scale [-1, 1, 1] }
            }
            object R2FootArchBlock { scale [-1, 1, 1] }
        }
        else {
            difference {
                intersection {
                    extrusion { path R2FootProfile  min Y -3.7  max Y 3.7  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
                    extrusion { path R2FootFront  min Y -6.4  max Y 6.4  rotate X -90 }
                }
                cube { scale [0.6, 1.4, 7]  translate Y 5.14 }
                object R2FootSideCut
            }
        }
        object R2FootArchBlock
        cube { scale [0.6, 1.55, 0.08]  rotate X 29.74  translate [0, 2.435, -5.458] }
        cube { scale [0.6, 1.55, 0.08]  rotate X -29.74  translate [0, 2.435, 5.458] }
        intersection {
            cylinder {
                min Y both == 1 ? -3.294 : 0  max Y 3.294
                scale [3.2, 1, 3.2]  rotate Z -90  translate Y 1.8
            }
            cube { scale [4, 1.6, 3.3]  translate Y 3.4 }
        }
        difference {
            intersection {
                extrusion { path R2FootSoleSide  min Y -3.7  max Y 3.7  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
                extrusion { path R2FootSoleFront  min Y -6.4  max Y 6.4  rotate X -90 }
            }
            object R2FootSoleSlots
        }
        material R2White
    }
}

// A shoulder's hub, the axle the leg turns on, in its own frame on the pivot's line: 2.84 across, reaching
// out through the horseshoe at the leg's top with a gap of 0.18 all round and stopping 0.2 short of the
// leg's face.  Its end is cut 0.5 deep with a round recess 1.68 across and four notches out from it, up,
// down, fore and aft, to 0.96 from its middle; in the recess a cylinder 1 across stands 0.2 short of the
// end, and four lugs from it reach into the notches, standing lower still.  From the fan-made droid's
// profile photograph.
R2Hub = group {
    difference {
        cylinder { min Y 8  max Y 12.4  scale [1.42, 1, 1.42]  rotate Z -90 }
        cylinder { min Y 11.9  max Y 12.5  scale [0.84, 1, 0.84]  rotate Z -90  material R2Dark }
        cube { scale [0.3, 0.96, 0.3]  translate X 12.2  material R2Dark }
        cube { scale [0.3, 0.3, 0.96]  translate X 12.2  material R2Dark }
    }
    cylinder { min Y 11.85  max Y 12.2  scale [0.5, 1, 0.5]  rotate Z -90 }
    cube { scale [0.095, 0.9, 0.18]  translate X 11.985 }
    cube { scale [0.095, 0.18, 0.9]  translate X 11.985 }
    material R2Metal
}

// The body's two shoulders, which the legs turn on: on each side a rounded boss standing out of the barrel
// to meet the leg, 6.5 across and swelling 1.3 toward the body at its middle, as the front elevation shades
// it; and the hub.  Both lie on the pivot's line, so they stay where they are as the body tips.
R2Shoulders = group {
    for side in [-1, 1] by 2 {
        group {
            intersection {
                sphere { scale 4.71  translate X 13.21 }
                cube { scale [0.75, 4, 4]  translate X 9.05 }
            }
            object R2Hub
            scale [side, 1, 1]
            translate Y R2Shoulder
        }
    }
    material R2White
}

// How high a foot's pivot stands above its bottom: the pin through the point of the fin under its leg, in
// the slot down the foot's middle, which the foot turns on and an outer leg leans about.
R2FootPivot = 4.5

// The stepped bracket on a leg's outer face, under the hub: a block standing 0.9 out of the face from just
// under the hub's ring to 23.2, its underside hollowed in a cove that sweeps back into the face by 22.4,
// and below that a box sloping out and down at 35 degrees, past the block to 14 in from his middle, with
// two slots in its end.  The front elevation gives the steps, the side elevation the widths -- the block 3
// fore and aft, its top cut in to clear the hub's ring.  In the leg's frame, its outer face toward +X.
R2BracketSide = path {
    move to -1.05, 25.66
    line to 1.05, 25.66
    line to 1.5, 25.06
    line to 1.5, 22
    line to -1.5, 22
    line to -1.5, 25.06
    close
}
R2BracketFront = path {
    move to 12.55, 25.66
    line to 13.5, 25.66
    line to 13.5, 23.2
    curve 13.003, 23.2, 12.6, 22.842 to 12.6, 22.4
    line to 12.55, 22.4
    close
}
R2Bracket = group {
    intersection {
        extrusion { path R2BracketSide  min Y -13.5  max Y -12.55  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
        extrusion { path R2BracketFront  min Y -1.6  max Y 1.6  rotate X -90 }
    }
    difference {
        cube { scale [1.15, 0.6, 1.3]  translate [0.56, -0.6, 0] }
        cube { scale [0.3, 0.3, 0.45]  translate [1.71, -0.6, -0.7]  material R2Dark }
        cube { scale [0.3, 0.3, 0.45]  translate [1.71, -0.6, 0.7]  material R2Dark }
        rotate Z -35
        translate [12.6, 22.3, 0]
    }
    material R2Blue
}

// The groove round the shank 14.92 up, 0.12 wide and 0.08 deep, in the shank's every face and the blue
// housing's: a thin slab less the shank and housing drawn in by the groove's depth -- the middle, as far out
// as the housing's face, and the rest, as far out as the sunken face.  The 1977 side elevation and a
// profile photograph of a fan-made droid put it at the same height.  In the leg's frame.
R2LegGroove = difference {
    cube { scale [1.85, 0.06, 2.6]  translate [11.35, 14.92, 0] }
    cube { scale [1.445, 0.1, 0.95]  translate [11.325, 14.92, 0] }
    cube { scale [0.8535, 0.1, 2.17]  translate [10.7335, 14.92, 0] }
    material R2Gray
}

// The piston hung from the bracket, down the leg's outer face into the top of the booster cover: a blue
// housing standing 0.3 out of the face, 2.06 fore and aft, rising out of the sunken face either side of it,
// its top tucked up inside the bracket's box, the groove round the shank cut across it and two more cut up
// its face either side of the rod from the cover to just short of that; and in front of it a silver rod
// 0.64 thick on a clevis under the box's end, flush with the cover's face where it meets it.  A photograph
// of the finished droid gives the parts and their colors, the side elevation their widths; the side
// elevation has the housing wider, 3 across, so its grooves -- 0.85 and 1.25 out from the middle there --
// are drawn in here to 0.56 and 0.8.  In the leg's frame.
R2Piston = group {
    difference {
        cube { scale [0.625, 4.2, 1.03]  translate [12.225, 16.8, 0] }
        object R2LegGroove
        cube { scale [0.08, 0.95, 0.05]  translate [12.85, 13.65, -0.8]  material R2Gray }
        cube { scale [0.08, 0.95, 0.05]  translate [12.85, 13.65, -0.56]  material R2Gray }
        cube { scale [0.08, 0.95, 0.05]  translate [12.85, 13.65, 0.56]  material R2Gray }
        cube { scale [0.08, 0.95, 0.05]  translate [12.85, 13.65, 0.8]  material R2Gray }
        material R2Blue
    }
    cube { scale [0.25, 0.55, 0.3]  translate [12.98, 20.05, 0] }
    cylinder { min Y 12.6  max Y 19.6  scale [0.32, 1, 0.32]  translate X 12.98 }
    cylinder { min Y 18.7  max Y 18.95  scale [0.39, 1, 0.39]  translate X 12.98 }
    material R2Metal
}

// The recesses in the horseshoe either side of the hub: two small ones behind it with a fitting each, and a
// long one before it holding a strut, all 1.15 across and 0.45 deep, 2.4 from the hub's line to 3.55.  The
// 1977 side elevation draws the two behind, a photograph of the finished droid the long one before; the
// legs mirror each other, as they do on screen (a fan-made droid photographed in profile has its left leg
// turned about, which makes the two legs one part to build).  A fitting is a round connector standing up
// from a recess's floor, short of the face.  In the leg's frame; `along` is fore and aft, positive behind.
primitive R2Fitting(up, along) -> group {
    return group {
        cylinder { min Y 12.15  max Y 12.48  scale [0.28, 1, 0.28]  rotate Z -90  translate [0, up, along] }
        cylinder { min Y 12.48  max Y 12.56  scale [0.13, 1, 0.13]  rotate Z -90  translate [0, up, along] }
        material R2Metal
    }
}
// The strut in the long recess, as the fan-made droid has it where the finished droid has three fittings:
// a short cylinder at its foot, a rod up from it with a nut halfway, and a block at its head with a stud
// above, all standing in the recess short of the face.
R2Strut = group {
    cylinder { min Y 23.1  max Y 24.2  scale [0.22, 1, 0.22] }
    cylinder { min Y 24.2  max Y 24.45  scale [0.19, 1, 0.19] }
    cylinder { min Y 24.45  max Y 26.1  scale [0.08, 1, 0.08] }
    cylinder { min Y 25.2  max Y 25.42  scale [0.18, 1, 0.18] }
    cylinder { min Y 26.1  max Y 26.7  scale [0.2, 1, 0.2] }
    cylinder { min Y 26.7  max Y 26.92  scale [0.17, 1, 0.17] }
    cylinder { min Y 26.92  max Y 27.1  scale [0.07, 1, 0.07] }
    material R2Metal
    translate [12.38, 0, -2.975]
}

// The booster cover over the outer side of an ankle, which the piston runs down into: seen from the side, 5.5
// fore and aft from the top of the ankle block, 12.8 up, to 10.4, narrowing from there into a disc 4.4
// across about 8.08 whose round bottom comes down to just over the foot, and stays clear of it as the leg
// leans about the foot's pivot -- a little smaller than the 1977 side elevation's circle,
// which a profile photograph of a fan-made droid shows is the cover's own bottom.  It stands 0.75 out of the
// face down to 10.6, as the front elevation draws it, then slopes in to a plate 0.25 out for the disc, which
// the ankle cylinder crosses; a slot is cut across the disc above that, as the photograph shows.  In the
// leg's frame, front to the right as the leg's profile.
R2CoverSide = path {
    move to -2.75, 12.8
    line to 2.75, 12.8
    line to 2.75, 10.4
    line to 2.2, 8.08
    curve 2.2, 6.865, 1.215, 5.88 to 0, 5.88
    curve -1.215, 5.88, -2.2, 6.865 to -2.2, 8.08
    line to -2.75, 10.4
    close
}
R2CoverFront = path {
    move to 12.55, 12.8
    line to 13.3, 12.8
    line to 13.3, 10.6
    line to 12.8, 9.6
    line to 12.8, 5.7
    line to 12.55, 5.7
    close
}
R2BoosterCover = difference {
    intersection {
        extrusion { path R2CoverSide  min Y -13.3  max Y -12.55  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
        extrusion { path R2CoverFront  min Y -2.8  max Y 2.8  rotate X -90 }
    }
    cube { scale [0.2, 0.07, 1]  translate [12.8, 9.25, 0]  material R2Dark }
}

// The fittings on the forward face of the block the blue housing runs down into: a blue cylinder 0.72
// across standing upright in a recess high in the face's outer half, its front just short of the face, and
// under it two gray plates over the face's lower part, 2.4 across together with a gap of 0.06 between them:
// the outer coming up to 0.1 short of the cylinder, with a bolt in its lower outer corner and a smaller one
// in its upper, and the inner stepping down 0.4 from it.  A photograph of the finished droid from his front
// and right shows them.  In the leg's frame, the face 2.65 forward of the leg's middle; the recess is cut
// from the leg.
R2CoverPlate = group {
    cube { scale [0.585, 1.2, 0.06]  translate [11.815, 8.8, -2.71]  material R2Gray }
    cube { scale [0.585, 1, 0.06]  translate [10.585, 8.6, -2.71]  material R2Gray }
    cylinder {
        min Y -2.86  max Y -2.77  scale [0.15, 1, 0.15]  rotate X 90  translate [12.1, 7.9, 0]  material R2Metal
    }
    cylinder {
        min Y -2.83  max Y -2.77  scale [0.1, 1, 0.1]  rotate X 90  translate [12.12, 9.72, 0]  material R2Metal
    }
    cylinder { min Y 10.1  max Y 11.25  scale [0.36, 1, 0.36]  translate [12, 0, -2.25]  material R2Blue }
}

// The box on the inner side of an ankle, which the hoses come from: gray, 2.55 across and 3 fore and aft,
// rounded top and bottom -- its top 9 up, its sides straight from 8.1 down to 2.4 and its bottom 1.3 up --
// with a band across its front under the round of its top.  Its lower half sits down in a scoop cut through
// the foot's inner front, so that it and the two fittings on its front show from in front down to their
// rounded feet.  The front elevation draws the box, the band and the fittings; a photograph of a fan-made
// droid from in front shows them rounded at the bottom and sitting in the scooped foot.  The elevation puts
// the box 0.9 further in and 3.1 across, where on this model its top would meet the skirt with him on two
// legs.  It belongs to the foot, so it stays upright when the leg leans.  In the leg's frame.
R2InnerBoxFront = path {
    move to 7, 2.4
    curve 7, 1.792, 7.571, 1.3 to 8.275, 1.3
    curve 8.979, 1.3, 9.55, 1.792 to 9.55, 2.4
    line to 9.55, 8.1
    curve 9.55, 8.597, 8.979, 9 to 8.275, 9
    curve 7.571, 9, 7, 8.597 to 7, 8.1
    close
}

// A fitting on the inner box's front, `at` across: a white bracket hooked over the band with a blue window,
// a gray bar down from it, and a dark rounded foot, which the hose leaves.  From the front elevation.
primitive R2HoseFitting(at) -> group {
    return group {
        cube { scale [0.28, 0.455, 0.075]  translate [at, 7.435, -2.675] }
        cube { scale [0.46, 0.765, 0.1]  translate [at, 6.215, -2.7] }
        cube { scale [0.22, 0.53, 0.015]  translate [at, 6.12, -2.81]  material R2Blue }
        cube { scale [0.18, 1.33, 0.06]  translate [at, 4.12, -2.66]  material R2Metal }
        cube { scale [0.335, 0.11, 0.125]  translate [at, 2.68, -2.725]  material R2Dark }
        cylinder {
            min Y -2.85  max Y -2.6  scale [0.335, 1, 0.335]  rotate X 90  translate [at, 2.57, 0]  material R2Dark
        }
        material R2White
    }
}

R2InnerBox = group {
    extrusion { path R2InnerBoxFront  min Y -0.4  max Y 2.6  rotate X -90 }
    cube { scale [1.275, 0.14, 0.05]  translate [8.275, 8.02, -2.65] }
    object R2HoseFitting(7.61)
    object R2HoseFitting(8.94)
    material R2Gray
}

// The scoop the inner box sits in: its outline drawn out by 0.12, cut through the foot from in front back to
// just behind the box.  In the leg's frame.
R2FootScoopFront = path {
    move to 6.88, 6.5
    line to 6.88, 2.4
    curve 6.88, 1.726, 7.505, 1.18 to 8.275, 1.18
    curve 9.045, 1.18, 9.67, 1.726 to 9.67, 2.4
    line to 9.67, 6.5
    close
}
R2FootScoop = extrusion { path R2FootScoopFront  min Y -0.52  max Y 7  rotate X -90 }

// The two hoses from the inner box's fittings to the foot's front, braided brass, 0.5 thick: each leaves the
// foot of its fitting going down and forward out of the scoop, arches up and out in front of the foot, and
// comes back into a dark round fitting on the plate on the foot's sloping front, square to it -- the inner
// fitting's hose to the upper one, arching to 4.3, the outer's to the lower, arching to 3.3 and further out
// in front, so that the two pass clear of each other.  The front elevation draws the loops and the two
// fittings on the foot's front, one over the other at its middle; photographs of the finished droid and of
// a fan-made one show them brass.  Each hose is drawn as two curves, each sampled at twelve points and joined
// by straight pieces, which round over at the joints and are far cheaper to trace than the curves.  In the
// leg's frame; the foot's front slopes back 29.74 degrees, 4.9 forward of its middle at 3.245 up and 5.73 at
// 1.795.
R2Hoses = group {
    cylinder {
        min Y 0  max Y 0.4  scale [0.32, 1, 0.32]  rotate X -60.26  translate [11.2, 3.245, -4.903]
        material R2Dark
    }
    cylinder {
        min Y 0  max Y 0.4  scale [0.32, 1, 0.32]  rotate X -60.26  translate [11.2, 1.795, -5.731]
        material R2Dark
    }
    tube {
        discontinuous
        radius 0.25 at [7.610, 2.350, -2.725]  radius 0.25 at [7.626, 2.199, -3.171]
        radius 0.25 at [7.674, 2.182, -3.607]  radius 0.25 at [7.752, 2.275, -4.027]
        radius 0.25 at [7.859, 2.456, -4.426]  radius 0.25 at [7.994, 2.701, -4.798]
        radius 0.25 at [8.155, 2.987, -5.138]  radius 0.25 at [8.341, 3.293, -5.440]
        radius 0.25 at [8.551, 3.594, -5.699]  radius 0.25 at [8.783, 3.869, -5.910]
        radius 0.25 at [9.036, 4.093, -6.068]  radius 0.25 at [9.309, 4.244, -6.166]
        radius 0.25 at [9.600, 4.300, -6.200]  radius 0.25 at [9.842, 4.294, -6.199]
        radius 0.25 at [10.066, 4.274, -6.196]  radius 0.25 at [10.272, 4.242, -6.184]
        radius 0.25 at [10.459, 4.197, -6.163]  radius 0.25 at [10.627, 4.140, -6.127]
        radius 0.25 at [10.775, 4.070, -6.072]  radius 0.25 at [10.902, 3.988, -5.996]
        radius 0.25 at [11.007, 3.893, -5.895]  radius 0.25 at [11.091, 3.787, -5.765]
        radius 0.25 at [11.151, 3.668, -5.602]  radius 0.25 at [11.188, 3.537, -5.402]
        radius 0.25 at [11.200, 3.394, -5.163]
        material R2Brass
    }
    tube {
        discontinuous
        radius 0.25 at [8.940, 2.350, -2.725]  radius 0.25 at [8.944, 2.201, -3.271]
        radius 0.25 at [8.956, 2.143, -3.806]  radius 0.25 at [8.979, 2.161, -4.321]
        radius 0.25 at [9.015, 2.241, -4.811]  radius 0.25 at [9.065, 2.367, -5.269]
        radius 0.25 at [9.133, 2.525, -5.688]  radius 0.25 at [9.218, 2.700, -6.060]
        radius 0.25 at [9.325, 2.876, -6.381]  radius 0.25 at [9.455, 3.039, -6.641]
        radius 0.25 at [9.609, 3.174, -6.836]  radius 0.25 at [9.790, 3.266, -6.958]
        radius 0.25 at [10.000, 3.300, -7.000]  radius 0.25 at [10.192, 3.285, -7.000]
        radius 0.25 at [10.367, 3.241, -6.998]  radius 0.25 at [10.525, 3.172, -6.989]
        radius 0.25 at [10.667, 3.081, -6.970]  radius 0.25 at [10.792, 2.971, -6.937]
        radius 0.25 at [10.900, 2.845, -6.886]  radius 0.25 at [10.992, 2.707, -6.814]
        radius 0.25 at [11.067, 2.560, -6.716]  radius 0.25 at [11.125, 2.407, -6.588]
        radius 0.25 at [11.167, 2.251, -6.428]  radius 0.25 at [11.192, 2.096, -6.230]
        radius 0.25 at [11.200, 1.944, -5.991]
        material R2Brass
    }
}

// The fin under a leg, which its foot turns on: gray, 0.9 thick, a triangle point down whose point is
// rounded about the foot's pivot down in the foot's slot and which flares up 3.1 to the leg, 4.6 long where
// it meets it, with the dark pin through its point across the slot.  Part of the leg, so it leans with it
// and turns in the slot about its own point.  `across` and `pivot` place its point; front to the right.
R2FinSide = path {
    move to -2.3, 3.1
    line to 2.3, 3.1
    line to 0, 0
    close
}
primitive R2Fin(across, pivot) -> group {
    return group {
        extrusion { path R2FinSide  min Y -0.45  max Y 0.45  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
        cylinder { min Y -0.45  max Y 0.45  scale [0.45, 1, 0.45]  rotate Z -90 }
        cylinder { min Y -0.75  max Y 0.75  scale [0.22, 1, 0.22]  rotate Z -90  material R2Dark }
        material R2Gray
        translate [across, pivot, 0]
    }
}

// An ankle cylinder, lying fore and aft about its own middle: a chrome tube 1.32 thick and 6.7 long, with
// caps a little fuller on its ends and an upright tab standing from the front one, and its middle sleeved
// in a blue band 2.6 long and square in section.  An outer leg has one across its booster cover's disc,
// 13.15 out and 7.93 up, and the center leg one either side of its ankle.  The 1977 side elevation gives its
// length, its height, its caps and the band; the front elevation the tab; the photographs its colors and
// the band's flat faces.
R2AnkleTube = group {
    cylinder { min Y -2.57  max Y 2.57  scale [0.66, 1, 0.66] }
    cylinder { min Y -3.35  max Y -2.57  scale [0.68, 1, 0.68] }
    cylinder { min Y 2.57  max Y 3.35  scale [0.68, 1, 0.68] }
    cube { scale [0.68, 1.3, 0.68]  material R2Blue }
    cube { scale [0.15, 0.1, 0.45]  translate Y -3.45 }
    material R2Chrome
    rotate X 90
}

// One leg and its foot, on the side `side` says: one for his left, which is on +X, minus one for his
// right, which mirrors it.  The leg leans `lean` degrees about its foot's pivot, its top going forward,
// while its foot stays flat; the whole is then moved back so that its hub is where it was, over z = 0.
// The profiles are drawn front-to-right; turned to stand up, the front faces -Z and the extrusion runs
// toward -X, which is why its span is written negative.
primitive R2Leg(side, lean = 0) -> group {
    return group {
        group {
            difference {
                extrusion { path R2LegProfile  min Y -12.6  max Y -9.8  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
                cube { scale [0.45, 0.6, 0.575]  translate [12.6, 26.4, 2.975]  material R2Gray }
                cube { scale [0.45, 0.6, 0.575]  translate [12.6, 24.4, 2.975]  material R2Gray }
                cube { scale [0.45, 2.2, 0.575]  translate [12.6, 25, -2.975]  material R2Gray }
                extrusion { path R2ShankSink  min Y -12.7  max Y -11.667  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90 }
                object R2LegGroove
                extrusion {
                    path R2HorseshoeGroove  min Y -11.727  max Y -11.607  rotate X -90  rotate Y 90
                    material R2Gray
                }
                cube { scale [0.45, 0.625, 0.3]  translate [12, 10.675, -2.65]  material R2Dark }
            }
            object R2Fitting(26.4, 2.975)
            object R2Fitting(24.4, 2.975)
            object R2Strut
            object R2Bracket
            object R2Piston
            object R2BoosterCover
            object R2CoverPlate
            object R2AnkleTube { translate [13.15, 7.93, 0] }
            // The fin behind the booster cover's disc.
            object R2Fin(11.2, R2FootPivot)
            scale [side, 1, 1]
            translate Y -R2FootPivot
            rotate X -lean
            translate Y R2FootPivot
        }
        difference {
            object R2Foot() { translate X 11.2  scale [side, 1, 1] }
            object R2FootScoop { scale [side, 1, 1] }
        }
        object R2InnerBox { scale [side, 1, 1] }
        object R2Hoses { scale [side, 1, 1] }
        material R2White
        translate Z (R2Shoulder - R2FootPivot) * sin(lean degrees)
    }
}

// The center leg's ankle, in its own frame with the center foot's pivot at its origin: a housing 4.5 fore
// and aft, tapering from 5.6 across at its top, 4.6 above the pivot, to a neck 2 across, 2.2 above it; an
// ankle cylinder either side, 1.75 out from the middle and 3 above the pivot, sunk into the housing's
// sloping sides; and the fin hanging from the neck into the foot's slot.  A photograph of a fan-made droid
// from in front shows the housing and the cylinders' capped and tabbed front ends either side of it, and one
// from his left side a cylinder with its blue band lying fore and aft right under the skirt.  The cylinders
// sit no lower so that, on three legs, their back ends clear the top of the foot.
R2CenterAnkleFront = path {
    move to -1, 2.2
    line to 1, 2.2
    line to 2.8, 4.6
    line to -2.8, 4.6
    close
}
R2CenterAnkle = group {
    extrusion { path R2CenterAnkleFront  min Y -2.25  max Y 2.25  rotate X -90 }
    object R2AnkleTube { translate [1.75, 3, 0] }
    object R2AnkleTube { translate [-1.75, 3, 0] }
    object R2Fin(0, 0)
}

// The center leg, in the body's own frame: its ankle, whose pivot -- the center foot's -- is `long` below
// the bottom of the skirt, and, if the ankle's housing does not reach up into the skirt, a strut the outer
// legs' shank is in section, 2.8 across and 4.5 fore and aft, from the skirt down to it.  On two legs the
// ankle is drawn up into the body, and only the fin shows under it.
primitive R2CenterLeg(long) -> group {
    strut = long - 4.6
    return group {
        if (strut > 0.01) {
            cube { scale [1.4, strut / 2, 2.25]  translate Y R2SkirtBottom - strut / 2 }
        }
        object R2CenterAnkle { translate Y R2SkirtBottom - long }
        material R2White
    }
}

// -- The droid -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// On three legs his body tips back about his shoulders and his outer legs lean forward about the pivots in
// their feet, which brings his shoulders down to `shoulder`; the center leg runs out until its foot stands
// flat on the ground -- its length worked out from the tilt and the shoulders' height, all three feet
// turning on pivots 4.5 above their bottoms.  At eighteen and fifteen degrees the center foot's heel stands
// 0.07 ahead of the outer feet's toes, as a profile photograph of a fan-made droid shows it,
// and the center foot's pivot is 3.8 below the skirt.  On two the center leg is drawn up until its foot's
// top is a tenth under the skirt, its pivot 1.3 below it, and the foot stands 2.15 off the ground.
primitive R2D2(legs = 2, dome = 0, weathered = 0) -> group {
    tilt = legs == 3 ? 18 : 0
    lean = legs == 3 ? 15 : 0
    ankle = legs == 3 ? R2FootPivot : R2SkirtBottom - 1.3
    shoulder = R2FootPivot + (R2Shoulder - R2FootPivot) * cos(lean degrees)
    long = (shoulder - ankle) / cos(tilt degrees) - (R2Shoulder - R2SkirtBottom)
    reach = (R2Shoulder - R2SkirtBottom + long) * sin(tilt degrees)

    return group {
        object R2Leg(1, lean)
        object R2Leg(-1, lean)

        // The body, the dome and the center leg, tipped back about the shoulders.
        group {
            object R2Dome { rotate Y dome }
            object R2Body
            object R2Shoulders
            object R2CenterLeg(long)
            translate Y -R2Shoulder
            rotate X tilt
            translate Y shoulder
        }

        // The center foot, the same as the others, stays level under the end of the leg.
        object R2Foot(1) { translate [0, ankle - R2FootPivot, -reach] }

        // Grime looked for a quarter of an inch about, which takes in his panel seams -- 0.08 wide and 0.06
        // deep -- and the edges of his fittings, and sand kicked up over his feet and lower legs as he rolls,
        // sixteen inches up at the most; nothing at all when he is clean.
        weather {
            grime weathered
            dust weathered * 0.8
            wear weathered * 0.5 to R2Aluminum
            splash 16 * weathered
            radius 0.25
        }
    }
}

// -- The X-wing ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Red Five, Luke's T-65B in A New Hope, as the 1977 studio model was built.  **It is written in meters**, unlike
// the droid: 13.4 m from the tip of its nose to the end of its exhausts, the length StarWars.com gives.  **+X is
// its nose, +Y up and +Z its port side**; the origin is the middle of its length, on the plane the wings fold
// to.  R2-D2 stands in the socket behind the canopy, his dome showing, scaled by 0.0254.
//
// Where the shape comes from: Heritage Auctions' photographs of the Red Leader hero model (one of the four
// built for the film, unaltered since; it stood in for Red Five as well), from above, below, behind, ahead and
// above, and the port side from below, as auction #7278 lot 89464 shows them.  They were measured together:
// each photograph's camera -- where it stood, how it was turned, its lens, and the middle of its picture, which
// a cut-out on a canvas does not keep -- and the ship's layout fitted to the places of seventy-odd features in
// all five at once, to two and a half pixels.  All five show one setting of the S-foils, 11.8 degrees open,
// which is taken as the attack position; none of them says where the wings turn about.  For that, a drawing of
// the ship from the reference books, which shows the closed wings dashed behind the open ones and draws them
// lying flat together, and the ESB Red Five miniature photographed nearly closed (lot 89513, July 2025), its
// cannons drawn together far closer than its engines.  The drawing names the parts -- the proton torpedo
// launchers low on the nose, the nose cone, the sensor window, the generators on the back, the S-foil
// actuators -- and is otherwise not followed: its views do not agree with one another, nor with the model.
// None of these is reproduced here.

XWingWhite = material { pigment [0.80, 0.80, 0.77]  diffuse 0.85  specular 0.12  shininess 25 }
XWingPale = material { pigment [0.70, 0.71, 0.70]  diffuse 0.85  specular 0.12  shininess 25 }
XWingGray = material { pigment [0.47, 0.48, 0.49]  diffuse 0.85  specular 0.15  shininess 30 }
XWingDark = material { pigment [0.17, 0.17, 0.18]  diffuse 0.8  specular 0.2  shininess 30 }
XWingIntake = material { pigment [0.26, 0.26, 0.27]  diffuse 0.85  specular 0.15  shininess 30 }
XWingFrame = material { pigment [0.60, 0.65, 0.68]  diffuse 0.85  specular 0.12  shininess 25 }
XWingRust = material { pigment [0.36, 0.20, 0.11]  diffuse 0.8  specular 0.1 }
XWingHole = material { pigment [0.03, 0.03, 0.035]  diffuse 0.6  specular 0.05 }
XWingMetal = material { pigment [0.32, 0.32, 0.33]  metallic 0.5  diffuse 0.6  specular 0.5  shininess 60 }
XWingGlass = material {
    pigment [0.08, 0.11, 0.11]
    transparency 0.55
    reflective 0.12
    specular 0.8
    shininess 200
}
XWingSuit = material { pigment [0.80, 0.33, 0.09]  diffuse 0.85  specular 0.05 }
XWingHelmet = material { pigment [0.86, 0.86, 0.84]  diffuse 0.8  specular 0.4  shininess 60 }
XWingBare = material { metal 'aluminum'  roughness 0.35 }
XWingVisor = material { pigment [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]  diffuse 0.4  specular 0.8  shininess 120  reflective 0.1 }

// -- Red Five's markings --
//
// The squadron's red, the same on every Red Squadron ship: a stripe down either side of the fuselage, just above
// its chine, from the wing roots to behind the nose cone, and on the outer face of every wing an L near its tip, a
// square at the leading edge with a bar running inboard along its back.  Behind the bar, just outboard of the
// engine, short stripes say which ship of the squadron it is -- StarWars.com: "painted stripes at the back of each
// S-foil to distinguish the starfighters from one another" -- as many as its number.  The proportions are those
// of the studio model seen from above in the trench, a still on StarWars.com, and of the Red Leader hero model,
// and the stripe down the fuselage of the Heritage photographs.  On screen the count changed from shot to shot,
// two, three or five, as the models stood in for one another; five is Luke's by the rule.  A few panel seams are
// drawn on the wings.
XWingRed = [0.50, 0.06, 0.04]
XWingSeam = [0.42, 0.42, 0.42]

// The L, in the wing's own X/Z: the square from the leading edge 1.2 back and 1.2 across, out to 4.53, and the bar
// 0.14 deep along its back, 1.1 further in.
XWingWingL = path {
    move to -2.40, 3.33  line to -2.40, 4.53  line to -3.60, 4.53  line to -3.60, 2.23  line to -3.46, 2.23
    line to -3.46, 3.33  close
}

// One of the squadron's stripes, 0.7 long and 0.16 across, about z = 0; they stand 0.26 apart.
XWingStripe = path { move to -4.45, -0.08  line to -3.75, -0.08  line to -3.75, 0.08  line to -4.45, 0.08  close }

// The wings' panel seams: two along the span, two across the chord.
XWingWingSeams = path {
    move to -2.94, 0.9  line to -2.92, 0.9  line to -2.92, 5.40  line to -2.94, 5.40  close
    move to -3.96, 0.9  line to -3.94, 0.9  line to -3.94, 5.40  line to -3.96, 5.40  close
    move to -4.80, 2.29  line to -2.40, 2.29  line to -2.40, 2.31  line to -4.80, 2.31  close
    move to -4.42, 4.59  line to -2.40, 4.59  line to -2.40, 4.61  line to -4.42, 4.61  close
}

// The stripe down the fuselage's side, as (X, -y): 0.25 deep, 0.02 above the chine, its front cut aslant.
XWingHullStripe = path {
    move to -1.10, -0.228  line to 1.15, -0.228  line to 1.93, -0.224  line to 2.69, -0.195  line to 2.95, -0.186
    line to 3.08, -0.180  line to 3.63, -0.154  line to 4.36, -0.152  line to 4.80, -0.155  line to 5.05, 0.090
    line to 4.36, 0.098  line to 3.63, 0.096  line to 3.08, 0.070  line to 2.95, 0.064  line to 2.69, 0.055
    line to 1.93, 0.026  line to 1.15, 0.022  line to -1.10, 0.022  close
}

// Dirt the `weathered` model carries as well as the weather's: streaks run aft over the wings and along the
// fuselage, as they did on the studio model.  Each is drawn three times, whole and two thirds and a third as
// wide, each layer a little dark over the last, so that it darkens toward its middle rather than ending in a
// painted edge; no two streaks of a layer touch, or their overlap would read as a hole.  Laid out once by a
// seeded draw.
